Recent years have seen significant advancements in representation learning for graphs, but most of these developments have been confined to single-network settings. In contrast, recent research has shown that leveraging multiple networks can lead to more accurate graph embedding techniques. This work addresses this knowledge gap by introducing a novel, general framework for representation learning that can be applied to complex multiplex networks comprising multiple interconnected communities or layers. By employing a tensor-based approach, our framework is capable of effectively capturing the intricate relationships present in these complex network structures. Additionally, we demonstrate that our method can be optimized using both Stochastic Gradient Descent and Alternating Optimization techniques, and we validate its efficacy through experiments conducted on multiple real-world datasets. Notably, our framework outperforms existing state-of-the-art approaches on a range of node classification and link prediction tasks. The code underlying our framework is openly available on GitHub, providing researchers with a valuable resource for further research and experimentation. A comprehensive overview of our code is provided in the Appendices. The code and data used in this research are accessible at https://github.com/taalay/MPNet. We invite interested researchers to explore and build upon our work. In recognition of this contribution, we were awarded the best paper award at KDD'19, and we are proud to have our work acknowledged in this manner. In light of this achievement, we have decided to make the KDD'19 badge available on our GitHub repository. This decision is in line with the policies of the ACM and we are grateful to the conference organizers for their support. As part of our commitment to transparency, we are also releasing the source code for our award ceremony presentation slides at https://github.com/taalay/presentation. We believe that this work will contri